<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 4.3 Partial Derivatives.

  Definitions, theorems, summaries, and problem statements are kept;
  derivations and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes, each
  with a one-line statement of what has to be done.  Content mirrors
  ch-14-3.ptx of the full lecture notes; every xml:id here is prefixed
  with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figures: all three are three-dimensional plots of a surface with traces
  and tangent lines on it, which set the geometry up rather than answering
  any exercise, so they are reproduced in full, as in the lecture notes.
  The reader is asked to read the slopes off them.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-fmv-partial-derivatives" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Partial Derivatives</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
State the definition of the partial derivatives
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m> and
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> as limits, and interpret each as
the slope of a tangent line to a curve cut from the surface by a vertical
plane.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Compute partial derivatives by treating all other variables as
constants, and use the standard notations <m>f_x</m>,
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>, and
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x_0,y_0)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Compute second partial derivatives, including the mixed partials
<m>f_{xy}</m> and <m>f_{yx}</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Apply the mixed derivative theorem to choose the more convenient order of
differentiation.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
A function of one variable has one derivative, which measures the rate at
which the output changes as the single input changes. A function of two
variables has two inputs, and so it has two rates of change: one obtained
by moving in the <m>x</m>-direction and one by moving in the
<m>y</m>-direction. These are the partial derivatives, and each is an
ordinary one-variable derivative in disguise, taken while the other
variable is held fixed.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-pd-definition">
<title>Definition of Partial Derivatives</title>

<p>
Consider the point <m>P(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m> on the surface
<m>z = f(x,y)</m>, and the two curves through it cut by the vertical planes
<m>y = y_0</m> and <m>x = x_0</m>. Each is drawn on its own below, with its
tangent line at <m>P</m>: <xref ref="fig-skel-pd-tangent-x"/> for the first
and <xref ref="fig-skel-pd-tangent-y"/> for the second.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-pd-tangent-x">
<caption>
The curve <m>z = f(x,y_0)</m> cut from the surface by the vertical plane
<m>y = y_0</m>, and its tangent line at <m>P</m>. The slope of that
tangent line is <m>\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="86%">
<description>
<p>
A grey surface with a green curve drawn on it through a magenta point P.
The curve is the trace of the surface in the plane y equals y nought, and
a blue tangent line touches it at P. A legend in the top left corner names
the surface, the curve, the tangent line and the point P.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont]
\begin{axis}[
  width=12cm, height=9cm,
  view={-35}{20},
  xmin=-2.3, xmax=2.3, ymin=-2.3, ymax=2.3, zmin=0, zmax=5.2,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none, clip=false,
  legend cell align=left,
  legend style={
    at={(0.01,0.99)}, anchor=north west,
    draw=gray!50, fill=white, fill opacity=0.92, text opacity=1,
    row sep=1.5pt, inner sep=4pt, font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont,
  },
]
% the surface
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, draw=gray!55, fill=gray!30,
          fill opacity=0.35, draw opacity=0.4, forget plot,
          domain=-2:2, y domain=-2:2, samples=15]
  {3 - 0.25*x^2 - 0.45*y^2};
% trace in the plane y = y0 (green)
\addplot3[very thick, green!50!black, forget plot,
          domain=-2:2, samples=33, samples y=0]
  ({x},{0.5},{2.8875 - 0.25*x^2});
% tangent line in y = y0, slope df/dx = -0.25
\addplot3[very thick, cyan!60!black, forget plot,
          domain=-1.3:1.3, samples=2, samples y=0]
  ({0.5+x},{0.5},{2.825 - 0.25*x});
% the point P
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.9, color=magenta, forget plot]
  coordinates {(0.5,0.5,2.825)};
\node[magenta, anchor=south west, font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont] at (axis cs:0.5,0.5,2.9) {$P$};
% the legend, built by hand so that the surface gets an entry too
\addlegendimage{area legend, draw=gray!55, fill=gray!30, fill opacity=0.35}
\addlegendentry{$z=f(x,y)$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, green!50!black}
\addlegendentry{$z=f(x,y_0)$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, cyan!60!black}
\addlegendentry{Tangent line in $y=y_0$}
\addlegendimage{only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.9, color=magenta}
\addlegendentry{$P=(x_0,y_0,f(x_0,y_0))$}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-pd-tangent-y">
<caption>
The curve <m>z = f(x_0,y)</m> cut from the surface by the vertical plane
<m>x = x_0</m>, and its tangent line at <m>P</m>. The slope of that
tangent line is <m>\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="86%">
<description>
<p>
A grey surface with an amber curve drawn on it through a magenta point P.
The curve is the trace of the surface in the plane x equals x nought, and
a dark red tangent line touches it at P. A legend in the top left corner
names the surface, the curve, the tangent line and the point P.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont]
\begin{axis}[
  width=12cm, height=9cm,
  view={-35}{20},
  xmin=-2.3, xmax=2.3, ymin=-2.3, ymax=2.3, zmin=0, zmax=5.2,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none, clip=false,
  legend cell align=left,
  legend style={
    at={(0.01,0.99)}, anchor=north west,
    draw=gray!50, fill=white, fill opacity=0.92, text opacity=1,
    row sep=1.5pt, inner sep=4pt, font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont,
  },
]
% the surface
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, draw=gray!55, fill=gray!30,
          fill opacity=0.35, draw opacity=0.4, forget plot,
          domain=-2:2, y domain=-2:2, samples=15]
  {3 - 0.25*x^2 - 0.45*y^2};
% trace in the plane x = x0 (amber)
\addplot3[very thick, orange, forget plot,
          domain=-2:2, samples=33, samples y=0]
  ({0.5},{x},{2.9375 - 0.45*x^2});
% tangent line in x = x0, slope df/dy = -0.45
\addplot3[very thick, purple!60!black, forget plot,
          domain=-1.3:1.3, samples=2, samples y=0]
  ({0.5},{0.5+x},{2.825 - 0.45*x});
% the point P
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.9, color=magenta, forget plot]
  coordinates {(0.5,0.5,2.825)};
\node[magenta, anchor=south west, font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont] at (axis cs:0.5,0.5,2.9) {$P$};
% the legend, built by hand so that the surface gets an entry too
\addlegendimage{area legend, draw=gray!55, fill=gray!30, fill opacity=0.35}
\addlegendentry{$z=f(x,y)$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, orange}
\addlegendentry{$z=f(x_0,y)$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, purple!60!black}
\addlegendentry{Tangent line in $x=x_0$}
\addlegendimage{only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.9, color=magenta}
\addlegendentry{$P=(x_0,y_0,f(x_0,y_0))$}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Write down the limit definition of each of the two slopes. (Both are the
ordinary difference quotient you already know, with one input held still:
for <m>\partial f/\partial x</m>, freeze <m>y</m> at <m>y_0</m> and give
<m>x_0</m> an increment <m>h</m>. Say which of the two figures each one
belongs to, and which variable is doing the moving.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5.5) -- (15,5.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {$\left.\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.2) {$\left.\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)} =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the limit definition of each of the two partial derivatives.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-pd-definition">
<title>The Partial Derivatives</title>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-pd-def-x" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{f(x_0+h,\, y_0) - f(x_0,y_0)}{h},
</md>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-pd-def-y" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{f(x_0,\, y_0+h) - f(x_0,y_0)}{h}.
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-skel-pd-two-tangents-video"/> builds both pictures up one piece
at a time <mdash/> the surface, the cutting plane, the parabola it cuts out, and
the tangent line to that parabola <mdash/> first in the plane <m>y = y_0</m> and
then in the plane <m>x = x_0</m>, closing with both in place. Use it to check
the two limits you just wrote down.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-pd-two-tangents-video">
<caption>
The two partial derivatives at <m>P</m>, built one at a time: the cut by
<m>y = y_0</m> with its green parabola <m>z = f(x,y_0)</m> and blue tangent
line of slope <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>, then the cut by
<m>x = x_0</m> with its amber parabola <m>z = f(x_0,y)</m> and red tangent
line of slope <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m>, and finally the two
together.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-pd-two-tangents"
       source="videos/pd_two_tangents_manim.mp4"
       width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>


<p>
In practice we never evaluate those limits. Say what we do instead.
(Compare <xref ref="eq-skel-pd-def-x"/> with the one-variable definition:
if <m>y</m> never changes, then <m>f(x,y_0)</m> is a function of <m>x</m>
alone. State the resulting rule for each variable in a single sentence.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {To find $f_x$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {To find $f_y$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, for the practical rule for computing each partial derivative.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-pd-as-ordinary">
<title>Partial Derivatives as Ordinary Derivatives</title>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-pd-as-ordinary-x" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \left.\frac{d}{dx} f(x,y_0)\right|_{x=x_0},
</md>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-pd-as-ordinary-y" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \left.\frac{d}{dy} f(x_0,y)\right|_{y=y_0}.
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
Here are some common notations for the partial derivative with respect to
<m>x</m>:
<md>
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
\qquad
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x_0,y_0)
\qquad
f_x(x_0,y_0).
</md>
Note that similar notation can be used for the partial derivative with
respect to any variable such as <m>y</m> and <m>z</m>.
</p>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-pd-examples">
<title>Examples</title>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-pd-paraboloid">
<title>Partial Derivatives of <m>1-x^2-y^2</m></title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> and evaluate the
following partial derivatives at the point
<m>(x_0,y_0) = (1/2, 1/2)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>f_x</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_y</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Do it twice. First straight from the definitions
<xref ref="eq-skel-pd-def-x"/> and <xref ref="eq-skel-pd-def-y"/>: freeze one
variable, give the other an increment <m>h</m>, and simplify the numerator
before dividing <mdash/> every term left carries a factor of <m>h</m>. Then
again by <xref ref="eq-skel-pd-as-ordinary-x"/> and
<xref ref="eq-skel-pd-as-ordinary-y"/>, holding the other variable constant, and
check the two routes agree. This is the surface of
<xref ref="subsec-skel-fsv-functions-two-variables"/>; finish by saying what
the two numbers say about it at that point, and why symmetry made them equal.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {A. From the definition, $f_x(1/2,1/2) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {B. From the definition, $f_y(1/2,1/2) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {The shortcut: $f_x =$ \quad $f_y =$ \quad and what they say:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for each partial derivative and its value, and for their geometric meaning.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-pd-quotient">
<title>A Quotient with a Trigonometric Term</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{2y}{y + \cos x}</m> and evaluate
the following partial derivatives at the point
<m>(x_0,y_0) = (0,0)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>f_x</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_y</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(For <m>f_x</m> the numerator <m>2y</m> is a constant, so only the
reciprocal needs differentiating; for <m>f_y</m> the whole thing is a
quotient in <m>y</m>. Watch that <m>\cos x</m> is a constant in the
second case but not the first.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9.5) -- (15,9.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.5) -- (15,6.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {A. $f_x =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.2) {\phantom{A. }$f_x(0,0) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.2) {B. $f_y =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {\phantom{B. }$f_y(0,0) =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for each partial derivative and then its value at the origin.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-pd-elliptic">
<title>Partial Derivatives as Slopes on a Surface</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the elliptic paraboloid <m>z = f(x,y) = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m>.
Evaluate <m>f_x(1,1)</m> and <m>f_y(1,1)</m> and explain what these
numbers represent.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Compute both partials as before. Then, for each, substitute the fixed
value to get the equation of the trace curve in that vertical plane, and
say which curve of <xref ref="fig-skel-pd-elliptic-x"/> and
<xref ref="fig-skel-pd-elliptic-y"/> it is. Compare the two slopes: one
tangent line is steeper, and the coefficients in <m>f</m> say why.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {$f_x(1,1) =$ \qquad trace in $y=1$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {$f_y(1,1) =$ \qquad trace in $x=1$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {What each number represents:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {Which is steeper, and why:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for each partial derivative with its trace curve, for the meaning of the numbers, and for the comparison of the two slopes.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-pd-elliptic-x">
<caption>
The trace <m>C_1</m> cut from <m>z = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m> by the plane
<m>y = 1</m> is the parabola <m>z = 2 - x^2</m>. Its tangent line at
<m>(1,1,1)</m> has slope <m>f_x(1,1) = -2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<description>
<p>
A tall dome shaped surface cut by the vertical plane y equals one. The
curve of intersection is drawn in dark blue and labelled C one, and a
magenta tangent line touches it at the point 1, 1, 1. A two-line legend
across the top names the surface, the cutting plane, the curve, the
tangent line and the point.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont]
\begin{axis}[
  width=10cm, height=9cm,
  view={-40}{16},
  xmin=-2.1, xmax=2.1, ymin=-1.6, ymax=1.6, zmin=0, zmax=5.7,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none, clip=false,
  legend cell align=left,
  legend columns=3,
  legend style={
    at={(0.5,1.0)}, anchor=north,
    draw=gray!50, fill=white, fill opacity=0.92, text opacity=1,
    inner sep=5pt, row sep=2pt,
    /tikz/every even column/.append style={column sep=7pt},
    font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont,
  },
]
% the surface z = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2 (only where z >= 0)
% parametrised over the elliptical footprint x^2 + 2y^2 <= 4, so the rim
% at z = 0 comes out smooth instead of stair-stepped
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=cyan!25, draw=cyan!55!black,
          fill opacity=0.42, draw opacity=0.35, forget plot,
          z buffer=sort,
          domain=0:1, y domain=0:360, samples=11, samples y=49]
  ({2*x*cos(y)}, {1.4142135624*x*sin(y)}, {4 - 4*x^2});
% the cutting plane y = 1
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, samples=2, samples y=2, domain=-2:2, y domain=0:3.6,
          fill=cyan!45, fill opacity=0.22, draw=cyan!50!black,
          draw opacity=0.35, forget plot]
  ({x},{1},{y});
% the trace C1: z = 2 - x^2 in the plane y = 1
\addplot3[very thick, blue!55!black, forget plot,
          domain=-1.41:1.41, samples=33, samples y=0]
  ({x},{1},{2 - x^2});
% tangent line at (1,1,1), slope -2 in the x-direction
\addplot3[very thick, magenta, forget plot,
          domain=-0.85:0.85, samples=2, samples y=0]
  ({1+x},{1},{1 - 2*x});
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8, color=magenta, forget plot]
  coordinates {(1,1,1)};
\addlegendimage{area legend, fill=cyan!25, draw=cyan!55!black,
                fill opacity=0.42, draw opacity=0.35}
\addlegendentry{$z=4-x^2-2y^2$}
\addlegendimage{area legend, fill=cyan!45, draw=cyan!50!black,
                fill opacity=0.22, draw opacity=0.35}
\addlegendentry{plane $y=1$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, blue!55!black}
\addlegendentry{$C_1\colon z=2-x^2$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, magenta}
\addlegendentry{tangent, slope $-2$}
\addlegendimage{only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8, color=magenta}
\addlegendentry{$(1,1,1)$}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-pd-elliptic-y">
<caption>
The trace <m>C_2</m> cut from <m>z = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m> by the plane
<m>x = 1</m> is the parabola <m>z = 3 - 2y^2</m>. Its tangent line at
<m>(1,1,1)</m> has slope <m>f_y(1,1) = -4</m>.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<description>
<p>
The same dome shaped surface, now cut by the vertical plane x equals one.
The curve of intersection is drawn in dark blue and labelled C two, and a
magenta tangent line touches it at the point 1, 1, 1. This tangent line
is noticeably steeper than the one in the previous figure. A two-line
legend across the top names the surface, the cutting plane, the curve,
the tangent line and the point.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont]
\begin{axis}[
  width=10cm, height=9cm,
  view={-40}{16},
  xmin=-2.1, xmax=2.1, ymin=-1.6, ymax=1.6, zmin=0, zmax=5.7,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none, clip=false,
  legend cell align=left,
  legend columns=3,
  legend style={
    at={(0.5,1.0)}, anchor=north,
    draw=gray!50, fill=white, fill opacity=0.92, text opacity=1,
    inner sep=5pt, row sep=2pt,
    /tikz/every even column/.append style={column sep=7pt},
    font=\fontfamily{phv}\selectfont,
  },
]
% the surface z = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2 (only where z >= 0)
% parametrised over the elliptical footprint x^2 + 2y^2 <= 4, so the rim
% at z = 0 comes out smooth instead of stair-stepped
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=cyan!25, draw=cyan!55!black,
          fill opacity=0.42, draw opacity=0.35, forget plot,
          z buffer=sort,
          domain=0:1, y domain=0:360, samples=11, samples y=49]
  ({2*x*cos(y)}, {1.4142135624*x*sin(y)}, {4 - 4*x^2});
% the cutting plane x = 1
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, samples=2, samples y=2, domain=-1.41:1.41, y domain=0:3.6,
          fill=cyan!45, fill opacity=0.22, draw=cyan!50!black,
          draw opacity=0.35, forget plot]
  ({1},{x},{y});
% the trace C2: z = 3 - 2y^2 in the plane x = 1
\addplot3[very thick, blue!55!black, forget plot,
          domain=-1.22:1.22, samples=33, samples y=0]
  ({1},{x},{3 - 2*x^2});
% tangent line at (1,1,1), slope -4 in the y-direction
\addplot3[very thick, magenta, forget plot,
          domain=-0.55:0.55, samples=2, samples y=0]
  ({1},{1+x},{1 - 4*x});
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8, color=magenta, forget plot]
  coordinates {(1,1,1)};
\addlegendimage{area legend, fill=cyan!25, draw=cyan!55!black,
                fill opacity=0.42, draw opacity=0.35}
\addlegendentry{$z=4-x^2-2y^2$}
\addlegendimage{area legend, fill=cyan!45, draw=cyan!50!black,
                fill opacity=0.22, draw opacity=0.35}
\addlegendentry{plane $x=1$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, blue!55!black}
\addlegendentry{$C_2\colon z=3-2y^2$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, magenta}
\addlegendentry{tangent, slope $-4$}
\addlegendimage{only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8, color=magenta}
\addlegendentry{$(1,1,1)$}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-skel-pd-elliptic-interactive"/> puts the two slices in one
picture you can cut open yourself: choose a plane, watch the parabola it leaves
on the dome, and see the tangent line appear at <m>P</m>. Use it to confirm the
comparison you just made <mdash/> the surface should fall away twice as fast in
one direction as the other.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-pd-elliptic-interactive">
<caption>
<xref ref="fig-skel-pd-elliptic-x"/> and <xref ref="fig-skel-pd-elliptic-y"/>, to
cut open. The slice <m>y=1</m> leaves the parabola <m>C_1</m> with tangent
slope <m>f_x(1,1)=-2</m> at <m>P</m>; the slice <m>x=1</m> leaves
<m>C_2</m> with tangent slope <m>f_y(1,1)=-4</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-skel-pd-elliptic"
             iframe="pd-elliptic-manim.html" width="100%" aspect="3:2">
<instructions>
<p>
The dome is <m>z = 4-x^2-2y^2</m> and the white point is
<m>P(1,1,1)</m>. Click <q><m>y=1</m></q> or <q><m>x=1</m></q>, or click a
parabola in the picture, to slice the dome with that plane: the parabola
cut out of it is drawn on the surface and the tangent line to that
parabola at <m>P</m> follows, labelled with its slope. Those slopes are
the two partial derivatives, and the legend collects them. <q>Along
<m>y</m></q> and <q>Along <m>x</m></q> turn the picture until the
<m>y=1</m> and <m>x=1</m> slices respectively are seen edge on, at their
true steepness; dragging turns it by hand.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-pd-second-derivatives">
<title>Second Derivatives and the Mixed Derivative Theorem</title>

<p>
We also define second derivatives such as
<md>
\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x \partial y},
</md>
by which we mean that we first differentiate with respect to <m>y</m> and
then with respect to <m>x</m>. We can alternatively denote such a second
derivative by <m>f_{yx}</m>. Likewise
<md>
\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2}
</md>
means that we differentiate with respect to <m>x</m> twice, and we can
alternatively denote such a second derivative by <m>f_{xx}</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-pd-second">
<title>Computing Second Partial Derivatives</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = \cos(xy^2)</m> and evaluate the
following partial derivatives.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>f_{yx}</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_{xy}</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_{xx}</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Mind the order: in the subscript notation the <em>left</em> letter is
differentiated first. Parts <alert>A</alert> and <alert>B</alert> start
from different first derivatives, so do them independently and then
compare the answers <mdash/> the comparison is the point of the exercise.
Both need the product rule at the second step.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,16);
\draw[gray!35] (0,12) -- (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8) -- (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,15.7) {First derivatives: $f_x =$ \qquad\qquad $f_y =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {A. $f_{yx} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {B. $f_{xy} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {C. $f_{xx} =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the two first derivatives and then for each of the three second derivatives.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-mixed-derivative">
<title>The Mixed Derivative Theorem (Clairaut's Theorem)</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>f(x,y)</m> and its partial derivatives <m>f_x</m>, <m>f_y</m>,
<m>f_{xy}</m>, and <m>f_{yx}</m> are defined throughout an open region
containing a point <m>(a,b)</m> and are all continuous at <m>(a,b)</m>,
then
<md xml:id="eq-skel-mixed-derivative" number="yes">
f_{xy}(a,b) = f_{yx}(a,b).
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Why should the order of differentiation not matter? There is a geometric
reason, with no derivative rules involved at all. Work it out. (Start at
<m>(a,b)</m>, step <m>h</m> in the <m>x</m>-direction and <m>k</m> in the
<m>y</m>-direction, and write down the four heights of the surface above the
corners of the resulting rectangle. Group them by the two edges running in the
<m>x</m>-direction: the difference of those two rises measures how <m>f_x</m>
changes as <m>y</m> increases. Then group the <em>same</em> four heights by the
two edges running in the <m>y</m>-direction instead. Both groupings collapse to
one four-corner sum <m>\Delta</m>; write it out and notice how it treats
<m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. Then let <m>h,k \to 0</m>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {The four corner heights:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {Grouped along the $x$-edges:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {Grouped along the $y$-edges:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {$\Delta =$ \qquad and letting $h,k \to 0$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the four corner heights, for grouping them along each pair of edges, and for the common four-corner sum and its limit.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-pd-mixed-commute-video">
<caption>
Why <m>f_{xy} = f_{yx}</m>: the four heights of the surface over the
corners of a small rectangle with sides <m>h</m> and <m>k</m>. Differencing
along the two <m>x</m>-edges (green, with blue rises) gives approximately
<m>hk\,\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\!\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
\right)</m>; differencing along the two <m>y</m>-edges (amber, with red
rises) gives approximately <m>hk\,\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\!\left(
\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)</m>. Both are the same four-corner
sum <m>\Delta</m>, so shrinking the rectangle to the point gives
<m>f_{xy}(a,b) = f_{yx}(a,b)</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-pd-mixed-commute"
       source="videos/mixed_partials_commute_manim.mp4"
       width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>


<p>
Check <xref ref="thm-skel-mixed-derivative"/> against your answers to
parts <alert>A</alert> and <alert>B</alert> of
<xref ref="ex-skel-pd-second"/>, and say what practical freedom the
theorem gives you.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {The two answers agree because:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {So in practice we may:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for why the two mixed partials agreed and one for the freedom the theorem gives.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-pd-mixed-shortcut">
<title>Choosing the Convenient Order</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider
<m>f(x,y) = \cos(x^2 y) + \dfrac{e^{(y^2+y+1)}}{y^2+5}</m> and calculate
<m>f_{xy}</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Do not differentiate in the order the subscript suggests. Look at the
second term: it depends on <m>y</m> alone, so one of the two orders kills
it immediately, and <xref ref="thm-skel-mixed-derivative"/> says you are
entitled to use that order instead. State which order you chose and why
before computing.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9) -- (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5) -- (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {Order chosen, and why:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {First derivative:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {$f_{xy} =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the order of differentiation chosen with its justification, for the first derivative, and for the resulting mixed partial.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>
